  "name": "Greek nisiotika (island music)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Greek nisiotika (\"island music\") folk-popular music tradition of the Greek Aegean Sea islands — Naxos, Mykonos, Tinos, Andros, Skyros, Lesbos, Chios, Samos, and adjacent islands — distinguished from mainland Greek demotic music by characteristic island-dance-rhythm repertoire (the syrtos, sousta, ballos), instrumental signature (lyra Cretan-style three-string fiddle on some islands, violin elsewhere, laouto long-neck lute, and santouri hammered-dulcimer), and the broader Aegean-island maritime-cultural orientation. The mid-twentieth-century commercial-recording era at Columbia, HMV, and Pathé Athens documented the canonical island repertoire. Distinguished from the parallel Cretan lyra tradition by nisiotika's broader-Aegean-island-network and lighter dance-emphasis register.",
